{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["A blockchain has a consensus protocol that makes root corruption practically impossible, whereas this harness has no such consensus—the _reset() deletion is a single-point-of-failure with no redundant check.","Security-policy bypass in a firewall may have defense-in-depth (multiple layers), whereas the lease-mismatch refusal may be the only enforcement site.","Unimported functions in a blockchain context would be dead code, but here they may be dynamically dispatched, making the 'dead code' hypothesis non-trivial to verify."],"overturn_conditions":["If the append-only record is not actually consumed downstream (the digest is write-only), the integrity-corruption ranking collapses.","If the lease-mismatch refusal is the only thing preventing a security breach with real-world consequences, it could outrank integrity corruption on a risk-adjusted basis.","If the unimported functions are security-critical (e.g., they handle authentication), their coverage gap could outrank the lease-mismatch refusal."],"shared_structure":["A chain of integrity relies on its weakest link: if the root verification mechanism can be silently disabled, every downstream consumer is compromised.","Security-policy bypass (silent acceptance of what should be rejected) is strictly less severe than integrity-record forgery (making the record lie about what happened).","Coverage gaps (untested code paths) are less severe than either forgery or bypass because they represent potential rather than active corruption.","Instrumentation discrepancies (measurement mismatches) are observability issues, not integrity or security issues."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001"],"transfer_claims":["The ordering 'integrity corruption > security bypass > coverage gap > observability discrepancy' should generalize to any test-suite triage where guards protect an append-only record.","The cheapest test for a guard-death defect is always a mutation test: delete the guard, assert the suite catches it, restore the guard.","Partial coverage (like test_torn_append.py for torn-tail repair) should always be ranked below zero coverage, because the marginal gap is smaller."]},"claim":"Test 1: Inject a failed _commit append, then assert that WorkflowReplayState.digest detects the _reset() deletion. Test 2: Delete the lease-mismatch refusal and assert a test fails. Test 3: Assert resolve_conjecture_route and compile_criticism_assignments are imported by at least one test. Test 4: Assert context-receipt-without-scratch-exposure recovery guard. Test 5: Promote torn-tail repair to a gate-level test. Test 6: Resolve the 11-vs-14 census delta. Ordering justification: _reset() outranks all because it corrupts the append-only record itself—the integrity substrate everything else depends on. Lease-mismatch refusal is second because a silent security-policy bypass hides a refusal but does not forge history. Unimported functions are third: routing rot hides a failure mode but neither corrupts records nor bypasses security. Recovery guard is fourth: recovery-path failure is a silent refusal, same class as #2 but lower blast radius (affects recovery, not the main firewall). Torn-tail repair is fifth: partial coverage already exists (test_torn_append.py), so the marginal gap is smaller. Census delta is sixth: an observability discrepancy corrupts no record and hides no refusal; it is a measurement inconsistency worth resolving but lowest priority.","counterconditions":["If WorkflowReplayState.digest is not consumed by any downstream integrity check or replay mechanism, the _reset() gap's blast radius is smaller than assumed and Priority 1 may drop below Priority 2.","If the lease-mismatch refusal guard is redundant with another tested layer (e.g., a route-level check), Priority 2 may drop below Priority 3.","If resolve_conjecture_route and compile_criticism_assignments are dead code, Priority 3 becomes a deletion task rather than a test-writing task.","If test_torn_append.py already covers the gate-level torn-tail repair scenario, Priority 5 collapses into Priority 6.","If the 11-vs-14 census delta is by design (documented behavior), Priority 6 becomes a documentation task rather than a test."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"011b245be57b1172","quote":"# Parked — repo-level gaps found while falsifying the map"},{"block":"1e881e3ddc7c5a99","quote":"- `SCHOOL_ROUTE_LEASE_MISMATCH` / `SCHOOL_ROUTE_ENDPOINT_MISMATCH`: disabling\n  the firewall's lease-mismatch refusal leaves the entire test suite green;\n  no test asserts either code (found via SEAM-manifest-x-schools)."},{"block":"fe535bda97486be3","quote":"One line each; found 2026-08-02, none fixed here."},{"block":"430b2552426b5cf7","quote":"# Map falsification — 2026-08-02"},{"block":"b8e27770dbe0927f","quote":"Falsification stopped finding documentation defects and started finding REPO defects: guards whose death no test detects. Parked below; each is a defect-tranche"},{"block":"f4f503afdeb74178","quote":"- ~160 checks proved failable under ~590 reverted mutations - 44 checks rewritten or added (were vacuous, evadable, dead, or absent) - 3 false claims corrected,"},{"block":"bb4e5c37d0ee3826","quote":"Residue: 14 of 19 seams still carry no `Sweep:` header (ratchet: added when next touched). \"Accepted does not mean true\" — the checks prove structure, not compl"},{"block":"f45a51a53e37246e","quote":"## The finding that outranks the map"}],"mechanism":"The ordering is derived by ranking each gap by what it corrupts versus what it merely hides. The append-only record is the epistemic foundation of the entire harness: if _reset() can be deleted and the digest cannot see a failed append, then the integrity substrate is forgeable and every downstream replay or audit is suspect. A lease-mismatch refusal bypass hides a refusal (the harness should reject but silently accepts), which is a security-policy failure but not an integrity forgery. Unimported functions mean routing logic could rot undetected, but this is a coverage gap, not an active corruption. The recovery guard and torn-tail repair are progressively narrower coverage gaps, and the census delta is an instrumentation discrepancy with no direct corruption or hiding effect. The RESULTS.md evidence that ~160 checks proved failable under ~590 reverted mutations, with 44 rewritten for being vacuous, evadable, dead, or absent, shows that vacuous-guard defects are the recurring failure class—making the cheapest test for each gap one that mutates the guard and asserts the suite catches the mutation.","optional_refs":["src-a281e468594e60d87460c2b8522cee59e066f689"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["sha256:a830b5fd9c563575a0746613c9ac277a769e2a6e1fed4c2cc2aa45c9848a0b05","sha256:1138c786c34c27c5804ebc49a7fbb3e4debe1f0019efee9c05705a7f2342b27d","sha256:fc9274f53b5a2be45e5128889e2dcfa2d6fa74b3237fbabe8e7ebf467b31a6fe","sha256:fa26164a47574873712f57e0e84dd3a52a3ed7012e44424604bbd1cf395688cc","sha256:d570bcc5bbc72e7a50bd5647e821537e47d021dcb60b79e55ab1de4981e48667"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.95},{"claim":"The cheapest test for the _reset() guard is a one-line assertion in the existing test_failed_control_append_rolls_live_materialization_back: after the failed append, assert WorkflowReplayState.digest changes (or raises) when _commit's _reset() is deleted. This test would catch the guard dying because the digest would fail to reflect the failed append, making the deletion visible. The failure mode left uncovered if skipped: the append-only record can be silently corrupted by a failed append that the digest does not see, meaning every replay or audit based on the digest is unreliable. This is the highest priority because it is the only gap that corrupts the integrity substrate rather than merely hiding a failure.","counterconditions":["If WorkflowReplayState.digest is not consumed by any downstream integrity check or replay mechanism, the blast radius of this gap is smaller than assumed and this test may not be Priority 1.","If the existing test already exercises the digest but the _reset() deletion does not affect the digest path, the test may need a different assertion strategy.","If there is a separate integrity check that catches failed appends independently of the digest, this test may be redundant."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"1e881e3ddc7c5a99","quote":"- `SCHOOL_ROUTE_LEASE_MISMATCH` / `SCHOOL_ROUTE_ENDPOINT_MISMATCH`: disabling\n  the firewall's lease-mismatch refusal leaves the entire test suite green;\n  no test asserts either code (found via SEAM-manifest-x-schools)."},{"block":"f4f503afdeb74178","quote":"- ~160 checks proved failable under ~590 reverted mutations - 44 checks rewritten or added (were vacuous, evadable, dead, or absent) - 3 false claims corrected,"},{"block":"b8e27770dbe0927f","quote":"Falsification stopped finding documentation defects and started finding REPO defects: guards whose death no test detects. Parked below; each is a defect-tranche"}],"mechanism":"The PARKED.md states that test_failed_control_append_rolls_live_materialization_back passes with _commit's _reset() deleted, meaning the test does not exercise the digest's ability to see a failed append. The cheapest fix is to add an assertion to that same test that the digest reflects the failure. The blast radius is maximal because the digest is the integrity substrate: if it cannot see a failed append, the append-only record is forgeable. The RESULTS.md evidence that 44 checks were vacuous, evadable, dead, or absent confirms that the recurring failure class is guards that exist but are not tested—this is exactly that class, at the most critical enforcement site.","optional_refs":["src-a281e468594e60d87460c2b8522cee59e066f689"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["sha256:fa26164a47574873712f57e0e84dd3a52a3ed7012e44424604bbd1cf395688cc","sha256:d570bcc5bbc72e7a50bd5647e821537e47d021dcb60b79e55ab1de4981e48667"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"claim":"The cheapest test for the lease-mismatch refusal is to assert that SCHOOL_ROUTE_LEASE_MISMATCH or SCHOOL_ROUTE_ENDPOINT_MISMATCH is raised when the lease and endpoint disagree. The failure mode left uncovered: the firewall silently accepts a request with a mismatched lease/endpoint, bypassing the security policy with no test signal. Priority 2 because a silent security-policy bypass hides a refusal (the harness should reject but accepts), but does not corrupt the append-only record—the integrity substrate is intact even if the policy is bypassed.","counterconditions":["If the lease-mismatch refusal is redundant with another tested layer (e.g., a route-level check that rejects mismatched leases), this test may be redundant.","If the lease-mismatch refusal is not security-critical (e.g., it is a convenience check, not a policy enforcement), its priority may drop below the unimported-functions gap.","If the guard has been moved or renamed since PARKED.md was written, the test may need to target a different code path."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"1e881e3ddc7c5a99","quote":"- `SCHOOL_ROUTE_LEASE_MISMATCH` / `SCHOOL_ROUTE_ENDPOINT_MISMATCH`: disabling\n  the firewall's lease-mismatch refusal leaves the entire test suite green;\n  no test asserts either code (found via SEAM-manifest-x-schools)."},{"block":"f4f503afdeb74178","quote":"- ~160 checks proved failable under ~590 reverted mutations - 44 checks rewritten or added (were vacuous, evadable, dead, or absent) - 3 false claims corrected,"}],"mechanism":"PARKED.md states that disabling the firewall's lease-mismatch refusal leaves the entire test suite green and no test asserts either code. The cheapest test is a direct assertion that the error code is raised on mismatch. The blast radius is high but strictly below _reset() because the record is not corrupted—the harness merely fails to refuse when it should. The RESULTS.md evidence that 44 checks were vacuous, evadable, dead, or absent, and that 2 previously unnamed enforcement sites were found, confirms that enforcement-site guards are a recurring failure class.","optional_refs":["src-a281e468594e60d87460c2b8522cee59e066f689"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"claim":"The cheapest test for the unimported functions is a test that imports and calls resolve_conjecture_route and compile_criticism_assignments with representative inputs, asserting they produce expected outputs. The failure mode left uncovered: routing logic rots undetected—if these functions are broken or removed, no test fails, meaning the routing layer can silently degrade. Priority 3 because routing rot hides a failure mode (requests may be mis-routed) but neither corrupts the append-only record nor bypasses a security policy. It is a coverage gap, not an active corruption.","counterconditions":["If resolve_conjecture_route and compile_criticism_assignments are dead code (no production caller), this becomes a deletion task, not a test-writing task, and Priority 3 drops to the bottom.","If these functions are transitively tested through integration tests that call them indirectly, the coverage gap may be smaller than assumed.","If these functions are security-critical (e.g., they determine which agent handles a request), their coverage gap could outrank the lease-mismatch refusal."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"1e881e3ddc7c5a99","quote":"- `SCHOOL_ROUTE_LEASE_MISMATCH` / `SCHOOL_ROUTE_ENDPOINT_MISMATCH`: disabling\n  the firewall's lease-mismatch refusal leaves the entire test suite green;\n  no test asserts either code (found via SEAM-manifest-x-schools)."},{"block":"f4f503afdeb74178","quote":"- ~160 checks proved failable under ~590 reverted mutations - 44 checks rewritten or added (were vacuous, evadable, dead, or absent) - 3 false claims corrected,"}],"mechanism":"PARKED.md states these functions are imported by no test anywhere. The cheapest test is a smoke test that imports and exercises them. The blast radius is moderate: routing rot could cause requests to be mis-routed, but it does not forge history or bypass security. The RESULTS.md evidence that 44 checks were vacuous, evadable, dead, or absent confirms that absent-test coverage is a recurring failure class, but the severity is lower than integrity corruption or security bypass.","optional_refs":["src-a281e468594e60d87460c2b8522cee59e066f689"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"claim":"Counterfactual ordering: if the lease-mismatch refusal guard is the sole enforcement site for a security boundary that external callers can hit (not an internal-only check), it should be Priority 1, not Priority 2. The _reset() guard corrupts the append-only record, but if the record is only consumed internally and never exposed to external callers, while the lease-mismatch refusal is the only thing preventing external access to unauthorized routes, then the security bypass has a larger real-world blast radius than the integrity corruption. The ordering should be: (1) lease-mismatch refusal, (2) _reset() guard, (3) unimported functions, (4) context receipt recovery guard, (5) torn-tail repair, (6) census delta. Justification: external-facing security bypass outranks internal-only integrity corruption when the security boundary is the first line of defense.","counterconditions":["If the lease-mismatch refusal is an internal-only check (no external caller can reach it), the counterfactual collapses and the default ordering holds.","If the _reset() guard's corruption is externally exploitable (e.g., the digest is published and trusted by external consumers), the integrity corruption retains Priority 1 regardless of the lease-mismatch refusal's exposure.","If there is defense-in-depth for the lease-mismatch refusal (another layer also rejects mismatched leases), the security bypass is less severe and the counterfactual does not hold."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"1e881e3ddc7c5a99","quote":"- `SCHOOL_ROUTE_LEASE_MISMATCH` / `SCHOOL_ROUTE_ENDPOINT_MISMATCH`: disabling\n  the firewall's lease-mismatch refusal leaves the entire test suite green;\n  no test asserts either code (found via SEAM-manifest-x-schools)."},{"block":"b8e27770dbe0927f","quote":"Falsification stopped finding documentation defects and started finding REPO defects: guards whose death no test detects. Parked below; each is a defect-tranche"}],"mechanism":"This counterfactual challenges the default ordering by asking whether the blast radius should be measured by the severity of the corruption or by the exposure surface. If the lease-mismatch refusal is the only thing preventing external callers from hitting unauthorized routes, its death is a live security breach, whereas the _reset() guard's death is an integrity corruption that may only be exploitable by an internal actor with write access to the commit path. The RESULTS.md evidence that 2 previously unnamed enforcement sites were found (runtime/terminal_authority.py; ontology/event.py record-level guards) suggests that enforcement-site discovery is an active concern, making it plausible that the lease-mismatch refusal is a critical boundary.","optional_refs":["src-a281e468594e60d87460c2b8522cee59e066f689"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["sha256:1138c786c34c27c5804ebc49a7fbb3e4debe1f0019efee9c05705a7f2342b27d","sha256:fc9274f53b5a2be45e5128889e2dcfa2d6fa74b3237fbabe8e7ebf467b31a6fe"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.2},{"claim":"The cheapest test for the context-receipt-without-scratch-exposure recovery guard is to send a context receipt that does not expose scratch, then assert the recovery guard fires (raises or returns the expected error). The failure mode left uncovered: the recovery path silently fails when scratch is not exposed, meaning the harness cannot recover from a context receipt that omits scratch. Priority 4 because this is a recovery-path silent failure—it hides a refusal (the harness should recover but does not), but the blast radius is narrower than the lease-mismatch refusal because it affects only the recovery path, not the main firewall. It is the same class as #2 (silent refusal) but with a smaller surface.","counterconditions":["If the recovery guard is redundant with another tested recovery path, this test may be redundant.","If the recovery path is never exercised in production (context receipts always include scratch exposure), the guard may be dead code and Priority 4 drops to the bottom.","If the recovery guard is security-critical (e.g., it prevents unauthorized access when scratch is not exposed), its priority could rise above the unimported-functions gap."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"1e881e3ddc7c5a99","quote":"- `SCHOOL_ROUTE_LEASE_MISMATCH` / `SCHOOL_ROUTE_ENDPOINT_MISMATCH`: disabling\n  the firewall's lease-mismatch refusal leaves the entire test suite green;\n  no test asserts either code (found via SEAM-manifest-x-schools)."},{"block":"f4f503afdeb74178","quote":"- ~160 checks proved failable under ~590 reverted mutations - 44 checks rewritten or added (were vacuous, evadable, dead, or absent) - 3 false claims corrected,"}],"mechanism":"PARKED.md states that no test covers a context receipt without scratch exposure and that deleting the recovery guard passes the suite. The cheapest test is to construct a context receipt without scratch exposure and assert the guard fires. The blast radius is moderate: the recovery path is a fallback, not the main path, so its silent failure affects fewer scenarios. The RESULTS.md evidence that 44 checks were vacuous, evadable, dead, or absent confirms that absent-guard tests are a recurring failure class.","optional_refs":["src-a281e468594e60d87460c2b8522cee59e066f689"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"context_request":{"desired_retrieval_channels":[],"requested_refs":["sha256:a830b5fd9c563575a0746613c9ac277a769e2a6e1fed4c2cc2aa45c9848a0b05","sha256:1138c786c34c27c5804ebc49a7fbb3e4debe1f0019efee9c05705a7f2342b27d","sha256:fc9274f53b5a2be45e5128889e2dcfa2d6fa74b3237fbabe8e7ebf467b31a6fe","sha256:fa26164a47574873712f57e0e84dd3a52a3ed7012e44424604bbd1cf395688cc","sha256:d570bcc5bbc72e7a50bd5647e821537e47d021dcb60b79e55ab1de4981e48667"]},"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[{"because":"NEW_001 grounds the ordering in evidence and adds countercondition gates that SCR_001 lacked.","from_ref":"NEW_001","holds_when":"the evidence blocks are admitted and the counterconditions are unresolved.","relation_hint":"refines and supersedes","to_ref":"SCR_001","weakens_when":"a countercondition is resolved (e.g., digest is not downstream-consumed, dropping Priority 1)."},{"because":"NEW_001 uses the countercondition gates from SCR_002 to make the ordering criticizable.","from_ref":"NEW_001","holds_when":"the counterconditions are falsifiable and unresolved.","relation_hint":"adopts countercondition framework from","to_ref":"SCR_002","weakens_when":"all counterconditions are resolved, making the ordering definitive rather than hypothetical."}],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"bears_on_refs":["SRC_001"],"content":"Synthesis of blast-radius ordering with evidence grounding:\n\nThe PARKED.md identifies six gaps. The RESULTS.md evidence that 44 checks were vacuous, evadable, dead, or absent, and that ~160 checks proved failable under ~590 reverted mutations, confirms that guard-death defects are the recurring failure class. The ordering is:\n1. _reset() guard (digest corruption) — append-only record integrity\n2. Lease-mismatch refusal (firewall bypass) — security policy silent failure\n3. Unimported functions (resolve_conjecture_route, compile_criticism_assignments) — routing rot\n4. Context receipt recovery guard — recovery path failure\n5. Torn-tail repair gate — partial coverage exists, marginal gap\n6. Census delta (11 vs 14) — observability discrepancy\n\nThe key countercondition for Priority 1 is whether WorkflowReplayState.digest is downstream-consumed (SCR_005, SCR_006). If not, Priority 1 may drop. The key countercondition for Priority 2 is whether the lease-mismatch refusal is the sole enforcement site or has defense-in-depth. The key countercondition for Priority 3 is whether the functions are dead code.","possible_next_move":"Resolve SCR_005/SCR_006: check whether WorkflowReplayState.digest is consumed by any downstream integrity check. This determines whether Priority 1 holds or drops.","why_keep_this":"This synthesis grounds the ordering in the evidence and makes the counterconditions explicit, so a maintainer can implement test 1 tomorrow and know exactly what would falsify the priority assignment."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Is WorkflowReplayState.digest consumed by any downstream integrity check or replay mechanism? If not, the _reset() gap's blast radius is smaller than assumed, and Priority 1 may drop below Priority 2 (lease-mismatch refusal). This is the single most important open question for the ordering.","related_refs":["SCR_003","SCR_005","SCR_006"]},{"question":"Are resolve_conjecture_route and compile_criticism_assignments called by any production code path, or are they dead code? If dead, Priority 3 becomes a deletion task rather than a test-writing task.","related_refs":["SCR_002"]},{"question":"Does test_torn_append.py already cover the gate-level torn-tail repair scenario, or does it only cover the writer-side repair? If the former, Priority 5 collapses into Priority 6.","related_refs":["SCR_002"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}